Well, there's no way to make "find" act like 'nix find; "find" is
really more like fgrep.  "findstr /R" behaves somewhat like grep.
(Use /? to get usage details for most Windows commands.)

this command string
DIR /S /B /A:-D C:\ |FIND "Nethack"
would return any files (/A:-D means no directories) with "Nethack" in
any part of the full pathname.

But really, if it were up to me, I'd do this:
1. try the current directory
2. try for a subdirectory "nethack" of the current directory
3. try the hardcoded c:\program files\Nethack
4. give up and tell the user to use a -d switch



